---@class mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutput
---@field awsIAMType? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputAwsIAMType Human-readable label that indicates whether the new database user authenticates with the Amazon Web Services (AWS) Identity and Access Management (IAM) credentials associated with the user or the user's role.
---@field databaseName mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputDatabaseName The database against which the database user authenticates. Database users must provide both a username and authentication database to log into MongoDB. If the user authenticates with AWS IAM, x.509, LDAP, or OIDC Workload this value should be `$external`. If the user authenticates with SCRAM-SHA or OIDC Workforce, this value should be `admin`.
---@field deleteAfterDate? string Date and time when MongoDB Cloud deletes the user. This parameter expresses its value in the ISO 8601 timestamp format in UTC and can include the time zone designation. You must specify a future date that falls within one week of making the Application Programming Interface (API) request.
---@field description? string Description of this database user.
---@field labels? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLabelsList List that contains the key-value pairs for tagging and categorizing the MongoDB database user. The labels that you define do not appear in the console.
---@field ldapAuthType? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLdapAuthType Part of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) record that the database uses to authenticate this database user on the LDAP host.
---@field links? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLinksList List of one or more Uniform Resource Locators (URLs) that point to API sub-resources, related API resources, or both. RFC 5988 outlines these relationships.
---@field oidcAuthType? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputOidcAuthType Human-readable label that indicates whether the new database user or group authenticates with OIDC federated authentication. To create a federated authentication user, specify the value of USER in this field. To create a federated authentication group, specify the value of `IDP_GROUP` in this field.
---@field roles mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputRolesList List that provides the pairings of one role with one applicable database.
---@field scopes? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputScopesList List that contains clusters, MongoDB Atlas Data Lakes, and MongoDB Atlas Streams Workspaces that this database user can access. If omitted, MongoDB Cloud grants the database user access to all the clusters, MongoDB Atlas Data Lakes, and MongoDB Atlas Streams Workspaces in the project.
---@field username string Human-readable label that represents the user that authenticates to MongoDB. The format of this label depends on the method of authentication: | Authentication Method | Parameter Needed | Parameter Value | username Format | |---|---|---|---| | AWS IAM | `awsIAMType` | `ROLE` | <abbr title="Amazon Resource Name">ARN</abbr> | | AWS IAM | `awsIAMType` | `USER` | <abbr title="Amazon Resource Name">ARN</abbr> | | x.509 | `x509Type` | `CUSTOMER` | [RFC 2253](https://tools.ietf.org/html/2253) Distinguished Name | | x.509 | `x509Type` | `MANAGED` | [RFC 2253](https://tools.ietf.org/html/2253) Distinguished Name | | LDAP | `ldapAuthType` | `USER` | [RFC 2253](https://tools.ietf.org/html/2253) Distinguished Name | | LDAP | `ldapAuthType` | `GROUP` | [RFC 2253](https://tools.ietf.org/html/2253) Distinguished Name | | OIDC Workforce | `oidcAuthType` | `IDP_GROUP` | Atlas OIDC IdP ID (found in federation settings), followed by a '/', followed by the IdP group name | | OIDC Workload | `oidcAuthType` | `USER` | Atlas OIDC IdP ID (found in federation settings), followed by a '/', followed by the IdP user name | | SCRAM-SHA | `awsIAMType`, `x509Type`, `ldapAuthType`, `oidcAuthType` | `NONE` | Alphanumeric string |
---@field x509Type? mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputX509Type X.509 method that MongoDB Cloud uses to authenticate the database user. - For application-managed X.509, specify `MANAGED`. - For self-managed X.509, specify `CUSTOMER`. Users created with the `CUSTOMER` method require a Common Name (CN) in the **username** parameter. You must create externally authenticated users on the `$external` database.

---Human-readable label that indicates whether the new database user authenticates with the Amazon Web Services (AWS) Identity and Access Management (IAM) credentials associated with the user or the user's role.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputAwsIAMType "NONE"|"USER"|"ROLE"

---The database against which the database user authenticates. Database users must provide both a username and authentication database to log into MongoDB. If the user authenticates with AWS IAM, x.509, LDAP, or OIDC Workload this value should be `$external`. If the user authenticates with SCRAM-SHA or OIDC Workforce, this value should be `admin`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputDatabaseName "admin"|"$external"

---List that contains the key-value pairs for tagging and categorizing the MongoDB database user. The labels that you define do not appear in the console.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLabelsList mongodb_atlas.ComponentLabel[]

---Part of the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) record that the database uses to authenticate this database user on the LDAP host.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLdapAuthType "NONE"|"GROUP"|"USER"

---List of one or more Uniform Resource Locators (URLs) that point to API sub-resources, related API resources, or both. RFC 5988 outlines these relationships.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputLinksList mongodb_atlas.Link[]

---Human-readable label that indicates whether the new database user or group authenticates with OIDC federated authentication. To create a federated authentication user, specify the value of USER in this field. To create a federated authentication group, specify the value of `IDP_GROUP` in this field.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputOidcAuthType "NONE"|"IDP_GROUP"|"USER"

---List that provides the pairings of one role with one applicable database.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputRolesList mongodb_atlas.DatabaseUserRole[]

---List that contains clusters, MongoDB Atlas Data Lakes, and MongoDB Atlas Streams Workspaces that this database user can access. If omitted, MongoDB Cloud grants the database user access to all the clusters, MongoDB Atlas Data Lakes, and MongoDB Atlas Streams Workspaces in the project.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputScopesList mongodb_atlas.UserScope[]

---X.509 method that MongoDB Cloud uses to authenticate the database user.
---
---- For application-managed X.509, specify `MANAGED`.
---- For self-managed X.509, specify `CUSTOMER`.
---
---Users created with the `CUSTOMER` method require a Common Name (CN) in the **username** parameter. You must create externally authenticated users on the `$external` database.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.CloudDatabaseUserOutputX509Type "NONE"|"CUSTOMER"|"MANAGED"

---@class mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettings
---@field autoScaling? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsAutoScaling Range of instance sizes to which your cluster can scale.
---@field diskTypeName? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsDiskTypeName Disk type that corresponds to the host's root volume for Azure instances. If omitted, the default disk type for the selected `providerSettings.instanceSizeName` applies.
---@field instanceSizeName? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsInstanceSizeName Cluster tier, with a default storage and memory capacity, that applies to all the data-bearing hosts in your cluster.
---@field regionName? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsRegionName Microsoft Azure Regions.
---@field backingProviderName? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsBackingProviderName Cloud service provider on which MongoDB Cloud provisioned the multi-tenant host. The resource returns this parameter when `providerSettings.providerName` is `TENANT` and `providerSetting.instanceSizeName` is `M0`, `M2` or `M5`. Please note that using an `instanceSize` of M2 or M5 will create a Flex cluster instead. Support for the `instanceSize` of M2 or M5 will be discontinued in January 2026. We recommend using the Create Flex Cluster API for such configurations moving forward.
---@field effectiveInstanceSizeName? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsEffectiveInstanceSizeName The true tenant instance size. This is present to support backwards compatibility for deprecated provider types and/or instance sizes.
---@field providerName string
---@field diskIOPS? number Maximum Disk Input/Output Operations per Second (IOPS) that the database host can perform.
---@field encryptEBSVolume? boolean Flag that indicates whether the Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) encryption feature encrypts the host's root volume for both data at rest within the volume and for data moving between the volume and the cluster. Clusters always have this setting enabled.
---@field volumeType? mongodb_atlas.CloudGCPProviderSettingsVolumeType Disk Input/Output Operations per Second (IOPS) setting for Amazon Web Services (AWS) storage that you configure only for AWS. Specify whether Disk Input/Output Operations per Second (IOPS) must not exceed the default Input/Output Operations per Second (IOPS) rate for the selected volume size (`STANDARD`), or must fall within the allowable Input/Output Operations per Second (IOPS) range for the selected volume size (`PROVISIONED` or `HIGH_PERFORMANCE`). NVMe clusters require either `PROVISIONED` or `HIGH_PERFORMANCE`.

