---Available in Public Preview: Field that represents whether your cluster is Atlas INFINITE or CORE. This is read-only and always returned in the response. It reflects the actual cluster state. This value matches `databaseEdition` if it was set, otherwise it reflects the default database edition assigned to the cluster.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805EffectiveDatabaseEdition "CORE"|"INFINITE"

---List of settings that represent the actual cluster state. This is read-only and always returned in the response. It reflects the current cluster configuration, which may differ from `replicationSpecs` due to system-managed changes.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805EffectiveReplicationSpecsList mongodb_atlas.ReplicationSpec20240805[]

---Cloud service provider that manages your customer keys to provide an additional layer of encryption at rest for the cluster. To enable customer key management for encryption at rest, the cluster `replicationSpecs[n].regionConfigs[m].{type}Specs.instanceSize` setting must be `M10` or higher and `"backupEnabled" : false` or omitted entirely.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805EncryptionAtRestProvider "NONE"|"AWS"|"AZURE"|"GCP"

---Internal classification of the cluster's role. Possible values: `NONE` (regular user cluster), `SYSTEM_CLUSTER` (system cluster for backup), `INTERNAL_SHADOW_CLUSTER` (internal use shadow cluster for testing).
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805InternalClusterRole "NONE"|"SYSTEM_CLUSTER"|"INTERNAL_SHADOW_CLUSTER"

---Collection of key-value pairs between 1 to 255 characters in length that tag and categorize the cluster. The MongoDB Cloud console doesn't display your labels.
---
---Cluster labels are deprecated and will be removed in a future release. We strongly recommend that you use Resource Tags instead.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805LabelsList mongodb_atlas.ComponentLabel[]

---List of one or more Uniform Resource Locators (URLs) that point to API sub-resources, related API resources, or both. RFC 5988 outlines these relationships.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805LinksList mongodb_atlas.Link[]

---Set this field to configure the replica set scaling mode for your cluster.
---
---By default, Atlas scales under `WORKLOAD_TYPE`. This mode allows Atlas to scale your analytics nodes in parallel to your operational nodes.
---
---When configured as `SEQUENTIAL`, Atlas scales all nodes sequentially. This mode is intended for steady-state workloads and applications performing latency-sensitive secondary reads.
---
---When configured as `NODE_TYPE`, Atlas scales your electable nodes in parallel with your read-only and analytics nodes. This mode is intended for large, dynamic workloads requiring frequent and timely cluster tier scaling. This is the fastest scaling strategy, but it might impact latency of workloads when performing extensive secondary reads.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805ReplicaSetScalingStrategy "SEQUENTIAL"|"WORKLOAD_TYPE"|"NODE_TYPE"

---List of settings that configure your cluster regions. This array has one object per shard representing node configurations in each shard. For replica sets there is only one object representing node configurations.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805ReplicationSpecsList mongodb_atlas.ReplicationSpec20240805[]

---Root Certificate Authority that MongoDB Atlas cluster uses. MongoDB Cloud supports Internet Security Research Group.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805RootCertType "ISRGROOTX1"

---Human-readable label that indicates any current activity being taken on this cluster by the Atlas control plane. With the exception of CREATING and DELETING states, clusters should always be available and have a Primary node even when in states indicating ongoing activity.
---
--- - `IDLE`: Atlas is making no changes to this cluster and all changes requested via the UI or API can be assumed to have been applied.
--- - `CREATING`: A cluster being provisioned for the very first time returns state CREATING until it is ready for connections. Ensure IP Access List and DB Users are configured before attempting to connect.
--- - `UPDATING`: A change requested via the UI, API, AutoScaling, or other scheduled activity is taking place.
--- - `DELETING`: The cluster is in the process of deletion and will soon be deleted.
--- - `REPAIRING`: One or more nodes in the cluster are being returned to service by the Atlas control plane. Other nodes should continue to provide service as normal.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805StateName "IDLE"|"CREATING"|"UPDATING"|"DELETING"|"REPAIRING"

---List that contains key-value pairs between 1 to 255 characters in length for tagging and categorizing the cluster.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805TagsList mongodb_atlas.ResourceTag[]

---Method by which the cluster maintains the MongoDB versions. If value is `CONTINUOUS`, you must not specify `mongoDBMajorVersion`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescription20240805VersionReleaseSystem "LTS"|"CONTINUOUS"

---Private endpoint-aware connection string that you can use to connect to this cluster through a private endpoint.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpoint
---@field connectionString? distilled.Secret Private endpoint-aware connection string that uses the `mongodb://` protocol to connect to MongoDB Cloud through a private endpoint.
---@field endpoints? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpointsList List that contains the private endpoints through which you connect to MongoDB Cloud when you use `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].connectionString` or `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].srvConnectionString`.
---@field srvConnectionString? string Private endpoint-aware connection string that uses the `mongodb+srv://` protocol to connect to MongoDB Cloud through a private endpoint. The `mongodb+srv` protocol tells the driver to look up the seed list of hosts in the Domain Name System (DNS). This list synchronizes with the nodes in a cluster. If the connection string uses this Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) format, you don't need to append the seed list or change the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) if the nodes change. Use this Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) format if your application supports it. If it doesn't, use `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].connectionString`.
---@field srvShardOptimizedConnectionString? string Private endpoint-aware connection string optimized for sharded clusters that uses the `mongodb+srv://` protocol to connect to MongoDB Cloud through a private endpoint. If the connection string uses this Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) format, you don't need to change the Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) if the nodes change. Use this Uniform Resource Identifier (URI) format if your application and Atlas cluster supports it. If it doesn't, use and consult the documentation for `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].srvConnectionString`.
---@field type? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointType MongoDB process type to which your application connects. Use `MONGOD` for replica sets and `MONGOS` for sharded clusters.

---Details of a private endpoint deployed for this cluster.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpoint
---@field endpointId? string Unique string that the cloud provider uses to identify the private endpoint.
---@field providerName? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpointProviderName Cloud provider in which MongoDB Cloud deploys the private endpoint.
---@field region? string Region where the private endpoint is deployed.

---Cloud provider in which MongoDB Cloud deploys the private endpoint.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpointProviderName "AWS"|"AZURE"|"GCP"

---List that contains the private endpoints through which you connect to MongoDB Cloud when you use `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].connectionString` or `connectionStrings.privateEndpoint[n].srvConnectionString`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpointsList mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointEndpoint[]

---MongoDB process type to which your application connects. Use `MONGOD` for replica sets and `MONGOS` for sharded clusters.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionConnectionStringsPrivateEndpointType "MONGOD"|"MONGOS"

---Advanced MongoDB process configuration options applied to the cluster.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805
---@field changeStreamOptionsPreAndPostImagesExpireAfterSeconds? number The minimum pre- and post-image retention time in seconds.
---@field chunkMigrationConcurrency? number Number of threads on the source shard and the receiving shard for chunk migration. The number of threads should not exceed the half the total number of CPU cores in the sharded cluster.
---@field customOpensslCipherConfigTls12? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls12List The custom OpenSSL cipher suite list for TLS 1.2. Requires `tlsCipherConfigMode` = `CUSTOM`; when `tlsCipherConfigMode` is omitted, supplying a non-empty list infers `CUSTOM`.
---@field customOpensslCipherConfigTls13? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls13List The custom OpenSSL cipher suite list for TLS 1.3. Requires `tlsCipherConfigMode` = `CUSTOM`; when `tlsCipherConfigMode` is omitted, supplying a non-empty list infers `CUSTOM`.
---@field defaultMaxTimeMS? number Default time limit in milliseconds for individual read operations to complete.
---@field defaultWriteConcern? string Default level of acknowledgment requested from MongoDB for write operations when none is specified by the driver.
---@field javascriptEnabled? boolean Flag that indicates whether the cluster allows execution of operations that perform server-side executions of JavaScript. When using 8.0+, we recommend disabling server-side JavaScript and using operators of aggregation pipeline as more performant alternative.
---@field minimumEnabledTlsProtocol? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805MinimumEnabledTlsProtocol Minimum Transport Layer Security (TLS) version that the cluster accepts for incoming connections.
---@field noTableScan? boolean Flag that indicates whether the cluster disables executing any query that requires a collection scan to return results.
---@field oplogMinRetentionHours? number|distilled.Null Minimum retention window for cluster's oplog expressed in hours. A value of null indicates that the cluster uses the default minimum oplog window that MongoDB Cloud calculates.
---@field oplogSizeMB? number|distilled.Null Storage limit of cluster's oplog expressed in megabytes. A value of null indicates that the cluster uses the default oplog size that MongoDB Cloud calculates.
---@field queryStatsLogVerbosity? number May be set to 1 (disabled) or 3 (enabled). When set to 3, Atlas will include redacted and anonymized `$queryStats` output in MongoDB logs. `$queryStats` output does not contain literals or field values. Enabling this setting might impact the performance of your cluster.
---@field sampleRefreshIntervalBIConnector? number Interval in seconds at which the mongosqld process re-samples data to create its relational schema.
---@field sampleSizeBIConnector? number Number of documents per database to sample when gathering schema information.
---@field tlsCipherConfigMode? mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805TlsCipherConfigMode The TLS cipher suite configuration mode. The default mode uses the default cipher suites. The custom mode allows you to specify custom cipher suites for both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.
---@field transactionLifetimeLimitSeconds? number Lifetime, in seconds, of multi-document transactions. Atlas considers the transactions that exceed this limit as expired and so aborts them through a periodic clean-up process.

---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls12Item "TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384"|"TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256"

---The custom OpenSSL cipher suite list for TLS 1.2. Requires `tlsCipherConfigMode` = `CUSTOM`; when `tlsCipherConfigMode` is omitted, supplying a non-empty list infers `CUSTOM`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls12List mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls12Item[]

---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls13Item "TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384"|"TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256"|"TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256"|"TLS_AES_128_CCM_SHA256"

---The custom OpenSSL cipher suite list for TLS 1.3. Requires `tlsCipherConfigMode` = `CUSTOM`; when `tlsCipherConfigMode` is omitted, supplying a non-empty list infers `CUSTOM`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls13List mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805CustomOpensslCipherConfigTls13Item[]

---Minimum Transport Layer Security (TLS) version that the cluster accepts for incoming connections.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805MinimumEnabledTlsProtocol "TLS1_2"|"TLS1_3"

---The TLS cipher suite configuration mode. The default mode uses the default cipher suites. The custom mode allows you to specify custom cipher suites for both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ClusterDescriptionProcessArgs20240805TlsCipherConfigMode "CUSTOM"|"DEFAULT"

