---Details of the failure that ended the shard key analysis operation.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisError
---@field code mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisErrorCode Reason that the shard key analysis failed. MongoDB Cloud may report additional values in the future without a new resource version, so treat a value this description does not list as a generic failure.
---@field message string Human-readable explanation of the failure. This message describes the classified failure and is not the raw error text from the cluster.
---@field retryStrategy? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisErrorRetryStrategy How to retry a failed shard key analysis. `BACKOFF` indicates that resubmitting the same request after a delay may succeed. `NONE` indicates that the failure describes the collection or the candidate shard key and that resubmitting the same request will fail the same way.
---@field retryable boolean Flag that indicates whether submitting the same request again may succeed. This flag is `false` when the failure describes the collection or the candidate shard key, because the same request then fails the same way.

---Reason that the shard key analysis failed. MongoDB Cloud may report additional values in the future without a new resource version, so treat a value this description does not list as a generic failure.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisErrorCode "NAMESPACE_NOT_FOUND"|"COLLECTION_EMPTY"|"SHARD_KEY_MISSING_SUPPORTING_INDEX"|"INSUFFICIENT_CARDINALITY_FOR_DISTRIBUTION"|"SHARD_KEY_CONTAINS_ARRAY_FIELD"|"SUPPORTING_INDEX_NOT_ASCENDING"|"SUPPORTING_INDEX_AMBIGUOUS"|"SHARD_KEY_ANALYSIS_TIMED_OUT"|"SHARD_KEY_ANALYSIS_FAILED"

---How to retry a failed shard key analysis. `BACKOFF` indicates that resubmitting the same request after a delay may succeed. `NONE` indicates that the failure describes the collection or the candidate shard key and that resubmitting the same request will fail the same way.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisErrorRetryStrategy "BACKOFF"|"NONE"

---Progress of one shard key analysis. Poll this resource until `status` reaches a terminal value, then follow `resultHref` to read the analysis.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisOperationResponse
---@field createdAt string Date and time when MongoDB Cloud accepted this operation (ISO 8601 format in UTC).
---@field error? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisError
---@field expiresAt string Date and time when MongoDB Cloud stops returning this operation (ISO 8601 format in UTC). Reads after this time return an HTTP 404 status. This value derives from `updatedAt`, so it advances with each state change while the operation is running and settles only once the operation is terminal.
---@field operationId string Unique 24-hexadecimal digit string that identifies this operation. When the operation succeeds, the analysis it created carries the same identifier.
---@field operationType mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisOperationResponseOperationType Kind of change the operation makes. Submitting a shard key analysis creates an analysis resource, so the operation type is always `CREATE`.
---@field resultHref? string URI of the analysis this operation created. MongoDB Cloud returns this parameter only when `status` is `SUCCEEDED`.
---@field retryAfterSeconds? number Number of seconds to wait before polling this operation again. MongoDB Cloud returns this parameter only while `status` is not terminal.
---@field status mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisOperationResponseStatus State of the shard key analysis operation. `PENDING` and `IN_PROGRESS` are non-terminal; `SUCCEEDED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED` and `SUPERSEDED` are terminal. MongoDB Cloud currently reports only `PENDING`, `SUCCEEDED` and `FAILED`. The remaining values are reserved and may be reported in the future without a new resource version.
---@field updatedAt string Date and time when this operation last changed state (ISO 8601 format in UTC). Once the operation is terminal, this is when it finished.

---Kind of change the operation makes. Submitting a shard key analysis creates an analysis resource, so the operation type is always `CREATE`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisOperationResponseOperationType "CREATE"

---State of the shard key analysis operation. `PENDING` and `IN_PROGRESS` are non-terminal; `SUCCEEDED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELED` and `SUPERSEDED` are terminal. MongoDB Cloud currently reports only `PENDING`, `SUCCEEDED` and `FAILED`. The remaining values are reserved and may be reported in the future without a new resource version.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisOperationResponseStatus "PENDING"|"IN_PROGRESS"|"SUCCEEDED"|"FAILED"|"CANCELED"|"SUPERSEDED"

---Completed analysis of one candidate shard key, describing how well the key would distribute the collection's documents and operations across shards.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponse
---@field avgDocSizeBytes? number Average size of one document in the collection, in bytes.
---@field correlationCoefficient? number Correlation between the candidate shard key and the order in which documents were inserted, from `-1` to `1`. Values near `1` or `-1` indicate a monotonically changing key, which directs all inserts to one shard. This parameter is absent when the shard key has no supporting index or the collection is clustered, in which case MongoDB Cloud does not compute it.
---@field createdAt string Date and time when this shard key analysis was requested (ISO 8601 format in UTC). This is when the analysis was submitted, not when it completed; the completion time is the `updatedAt` of the operation that produced it.
---@field expiresAt string Date and time when MongoDB Cloud stops returning this shard key analysis (ISO 8601 format in UTC). Reads after this time return an HTTP 404 status.
---@field id string Unique 24-hexadecimal digit string that identifies this shard key analysis. This is the same identifier as the `operationId` of the operation that produced it.
---@field isUnique? boolean Flag that indicates whether the index supporting the candidate shard key enforces a uniqueness constraint.
---@field monotonicity? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseMonotonicity Whether the shard key value increases or decreases with the order in which documents were inserted. A monotonically changing shard key directs all inserts to one shard. `UNKNOWN` indicates that MongoDB Cloud could not determine monotonicity, which happens when the shard key has no supporting index or the collection is clustered.
---@field mostCommonValues? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseMostCommonValuesList Shard key values that occur most frequently in the sampled documents, with how often each occurs. MongoDB Cloud returns this parameter only to callers that can read the collection's data, because it carries values out of the collection rather than statistics about them.
---@field namespace string Combination of the database and collection that was analyzed, written as `<database>.<collection>`.
---@field note? string Guidance from MongoDB on how to interpret the metrics in this analysis. This parameter is absent when the analysis carries no guidance.
---@field numDistinctValues? number Number of distinct values that the candidate shard key takes across the sampled documents. A value close to `numDocsSampled` indicates high cardinality, which distributes documents well.
---@field numDocsSampled? number Number of documents that MongoDB Cloud sampled to compute these metrics.
---@field numDocsTotal? number Number of documents in the collection.
---@field numOrphanDocs? number Number of orphaned documents in the collection. This parameter is absent for collections that have none.
---@field percentageOfMultiShardReads? number Percentage of sampled reads that the candidate shard key would route to more than one shard but not to every shard.
---@field percentageOfMultiShardWrites? number Percentage of sampled writes that the candidate shard key would route to more than one shard but not to every shard.
---@field percentageOfScatterGatherReads? number Percentage of sampled reads that the candidate shard key would broadcast to every shard. Lower is better.
---@field percentageOfScatterGatherWrites? number Percentage of sampled writes that the candidate shard key would broadcast to every shard. Lower is better.
---@field percentageOfSingleShardReads? number Percentage of sampled reads that the candidate shard key would route to exactly one shard. Higher is better.
---@field percentageOfSingleShardWrites? number Percentage of sampled writes that the candidate shard key would route to exactly one shard. Higher is better.
---@field rawOutput? string Unmodified result of the `analyzeShardKey` command, as a JSON string. Its content tracks the MongoDB server version of the analyzed cluster rather than the version of this resource, so members outside the parameters above may appear or change without a new resource version. Do not parse it as a stable contract. `mostCommonValues` is removed from it for callers that cannot read the collection's data. This parameter is absent if MongoDB Cloud could not serialize the command result, which costs the raw view but not the analysis.
---@field readSampleSize? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyReadSampleSize
---@field shardKey? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseShardKeyList Candidate shard key that was analyzed, given as one object per shard key field in the order the fields form the key. This parameter is absent when MongoDB Cloud has no shard key recorded for the analysis.
---@field writeSampleSize? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyWriteSampleSize

---Whether the shard key value increases or decreases with the order in which documents were inserted. A monotonically changing shard key directs all inserts to one shard. `UNKNOWN` indicates that MongoDB Cloud could not determine monotonicity, which happens when the shard key has no supporting index or the collection is clustered.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseMonotonicity "MONOTONIC"|"NOT_MONOTONIC"|"UNKNOWN"

---Shard key values that occur most frequently in the sampled documents, with how often each occurs. MongoDB Cloud returns this parameter only to callers that can read the collection's data, because it carries values out of the collection rather than statistics about them.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseMostCommonValuesList mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyMostCommonValue[]

---Key-value pair that sets one shard key field as the key and how it was indexed as its value: `1` for an ascending range-based field, `hashed` for a hashed field.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseShardKeyItemMap { [string]: string }

---Candidate shard key that was analyzed, given as one object per shard key field in the order the fields form the key. This parameter is absent when MongoDB Cloud has no shard key recorded for the analysis.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseShardKeyList mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyAnalysisResponseShardKeyItemMap[]

---Shard key value that occurs frequently in the sampled documents, with the number of sampled documents that carry it.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyMostCommonValue
---@field frequency? number Number of sampled documents that carry this shard key value.
---@field truncated? boolean Flag that indicates whether MongoDB Cloud omitted `value` because it was too large to return. When this flag is `true`, `frequency` still describes a real shard key value even though the value itself is absent.
---@field value? mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyMostCommonValueValueMap Shard key value, given as one field-value pair per shard key field. This parameter is absent when `truncated` is `true`.

---Shard key value, given as one field-value pair per shard key field. This parameter is absent when `truncated` is `true`.
---@alias mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyMostCommonValueValueMap { [string]: any }

---Number of sampled read operations that MongoDB Cloud used to compute the read distribution, broken down by the command that issued them.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyReadSampleSize
---@field aggregate? number Number of sampled `aggregate` operations.
---@field count? number Number of sampled `count` operations.
---@field distinct? number Number of sampled `distinct` operations.
---@field find? number Number of sampled `find` operations.
---@field total? number Total number of sampled read operations.

---Number of sampled write operations that MongoDB Cloud used to compute the write distribution, broken down by the command that issued them.
---@class mongodb_atlas.ShardKeyWriteSampleSize
---@field delete? number Number of sampled `delete` operations.
---@field findAndModify? number Number of sampled `findAndModify` operations.
